1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:04,170
Resgrid Dispatch brings calls, units,
personnel, the map,

2
00:00:04,309 --> 00:00:06,190
and notes into one console.

3
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:10,130
This walkthrough is for dispatchers and
other authorized coordinators

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:13,689
who need to find the right resource and
maintain its recorded state.

5
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,889
We use a fictional firehouse, but the same
console pattern can support

6
00:00:17,889 --> 00:00:22,709
community response teams, search and
rescue, security operations, wildland

7
00:00:22,709 --> 00:00:25,229
contractors, and industrial response.

8
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:28,949
Start in the call panel and enter
Community in its search field.

9
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:32,410
The list now shows the fictional community
medical standby.

10
00:00:33,039 --> 00:00:36,920
Notice that the top summary still reports
three active calls while

11
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:38,500
the searched panel shows one.

12
00:00:38,939 --> 00:00:42,419
Search helps find an incident in the list;
check the incident

13
00:00:42,419 --> 00:00:43,849
name before selecting it.

14
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:45,820
Select the intended call card.

15
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:51,599
The blue Filtering by call banner
identifies DEMO-0002, and the

16
00:00:51,599 --> 00:00:55,540
resource panels now say Units on Call and
Personnel on Call.

17
00:00:55,989 --> 00:00:58,170
Call Notes is also scoped to that
incident.

18
00:00:58,649 --> 00:01:00,510
Read this banner before acting.

19
00:01:00,930 --> 00:01:03,860
An On Call assignment label and the
resource status are

20
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:05,590
separate pieces of information.

21
00:01:05,759 --> 00:01:08,650
Use Clear Filter to leave the incident
context.

22
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,560
The banner is gone and the resource
headings return to

23
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:13,740
Units, Personnel, and Notes.

24
00:01:14,119 --> 00:01:18,110
Community is still in the call search
field, so that panel remains narrowed.

25
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,449
Clear its search separately when you want
the full call list.

26
00:01:21,790 --> 00:01:25,409
This distinction helps explain why a panel
can still look filtered

27
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:26,870
after leaving an incident.

28
00:01:26,989 --> 00:01:31,079
With the call search cleared, turn on
Available only at the top.

29
00:01:31,599 --> 00:01:36,270
In this recorded example, the unit panel
contains four units instead of six.

30
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,319
Engine 51 is listed as Available.

31
00:01:39,810 --> 00:01:43,289
Read the resulting list and its count when
filtering resources.

32
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:47,640
A recorded availability value still needs
to be interpreted using your

33
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:49,599
organization’s dispatch procedure.

34
00:01:49,709 --> 00:01:54,129
Turn Available only off, then enter Engine
in the unit search.

35
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:59,509
Two matches are visible: Engine 51, marked
Available, and Reserve

36
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:02,060
Engine 51, marked Out of Service.

37
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,900
Check the full unit name and displayed
state.

38
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:08,960
Similar names make it especially useful to
select from the unit panel

39
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:13,370
deliberately and verify the selected
resource before submitting an update.

40
00:02:13,519 --> 00:02:16,170
Select Engine 51 in the unit panel.

41
00:02:16,650 --> 00:02:21,300
Its Actions area now identifies Engine 51
and exposes Status,

42
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,269
Select Status, and Note.

43
00:02:23,629 --> 00:02:26,019
Check that identity before entering an
update.

44
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:28,349
This is the unit action form.

45
00:02:28,759 --> 00:02:32,120
Leave unrelated fields alone when the task
only requires a

46
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:33,940
status and its supporting note.

47
00:02:34,119 --> 00:02:35,589
Open Select Status.

48
00:02:35,999 --> 00:02:41,599
The displayed choices include Responding,
Available, Committed, and On Scene.

49
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:46,229
The chooser also describes their
destination support: Responding shows

50
00:02:46,229 --> 00:02:51,209
Call destination, while Available shows
Station destination in this department.

51
00:02:51,649 --> 00:02:54,139
Choose the state that matches the intended
action.

52
00:02:54,510 --> 00:02:57,350
For this software exercise, we choose
Available.

53
00:02:57,590 --> 00:03:00,379
Open Destination and review the current
selection.

54
00:03:00,809 --> 00:03:02,519
No Destination is selected.

55
00:03:02,969 --> 00:03:07,749
The Stations section contains rows named
No Station and Firehouse 51.

56
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:12,259
A row named No Station and the separate No
Destination choice are

57
00:03:12,259 --> 00:03:14,169
distinct entries in this example.

58
00:03:14,489 --> 00:03:17,019
We retain No Destination for the exercise.

59
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:21,079
Check the selected destination before
sending any status update.

60
00:03:21,330 --> 00:03:27,029
Review the prepared values: Engine 51,
Available, and No Destination.

61
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:31,389
The note says Training only, Dispatch
status practice.

62
00:03:31,819 --> 00:03:36,279
Once the intended resource and values are
correct, choose Update Status.

63
00:03:36,759 --> 00:03:40,140
The note provides context for this
fictional software exercise.

64
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:44,099
For actual operations, enter the
information your organization

65
00:03:44,099 --> 00:03:45,870
requires for the status change.

66
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:50,699
After submission, the Activity Log reports
Unit status updated, and

67
00:03:50,869 --> 00:03:53,530
Engine 51 still displays Available.

68
00:03:54,010 --> 00:03:57,850
We deliberately submitted the existing
Available value as practice.

69
00:03:58,210 --> 00:04:01,359
Read the result rather than expecting a
different status color.

70
00:04:01,859 --> 00:04:06,209
Next, inspect the unit’s separate details
page to check the stored values.

71
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:09,850
Open Engine 51’s details using its details
icon.

72
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:15,339
Status Information shows Available,
Destination shows None, and Status

73
00:04:15,389 --> 00:04:17,649
Updated provides a recorded timestamp.

74
00:04:18,139 --> 00:04:20,790
Assigned Roles still lists Christopher
Herrmann.

75
00:04:21,259 --> 00:04:25,020
This readback lets a coordinator check the
saved unit state and see the

76
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:26,879
crew assignment on the same record.

77
00:04:27,039 --> 00:04:30,059
Scroll within the same unit details page
to Note.

78
00:04:30,559 --> 00:04:34,250
The exact training note is now visible as
stored record text.

79
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:36,970
Compare it with the note entered before
submission.

80
00:04:37,430 --> 00:04:40,819
Checking the saved note is useful when
another coordinator needs to

81
00:04:40,819 --> 00:04:42,850
understand the context of an update.

82
00:04:43,019 --> 00:04:46,329
Return to the console and search for Boden
in Personnel.

83
00:04:46,799 --> 00:04:50,499
The matching Wallace Boden card shows
Command and Chief, along

84
00:04:50,499 --> 00:04:52,880
with Standing By and Available.

85
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,590
Read these fields separately: the card
presents a response

86
00:04:56,590 --> 00:04:58,510
status and a staffing value.

87
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,919
Confirm the person’s name before opening
their actions.

88
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:03,580
Select Wallace Boden.

89
00:05:04,089 --> 00:05:08,690
The Actions area now identifies that
person and shows the response-status form

90
00:05:08,690 --> 00:05:10,840
with Select Status and its note field.

91
00:05:11,229 --> 00:05:14,010
Staffing has its own controls lower in
this area.

92
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:18,019
For this exercise, leave the
response-status form unchanged and

93
00:05:18,019 --> 00:05:19,870
continue to the staffing selector.

94
00:05:20,039 --> 00:05:21,720
Open Select Staffing Level.

95
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:27,690
This department offers Available, Delayed,
Unavailable, Committed, and On Shift.

96
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:31,390
These are the staffing choices shown by
this training configuration.

97
00:05:31,869 --> 00:05:34,649
Choose the level your organization intends
to record.

98
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:39,870
Our exercise uses Available, preserving
the member’s existing response status.

99
00:05:40,059 --> 00:05:44,199
Review Wallace Boden, Staffing Available,
and the note Training

100
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:46,460
only, Dispatch staffing practice.

101
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:49,829
Choose Update Staffing when those values
are correct.

102
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:53,720
Check the action’s label before
submitting: this exercise

103
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:55,100
sends the staffing form.

104
00:05:55,430 --> 00:05:58,879
It does not submit the separate
response-status form above it.

105
00:05:58,989 --> 00:06:02,630
The Activity Log now reports Personnel
staffing updated.

106
00:06:03,050 --> 00:06:06,309
Wallace Boden’s card shows Standing By and
Available.

107
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:10,399
Read both values after submission, then
use the personnel details

108
00:06:10,399 --> 00:06:11,689
view for a separate readback.

109
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:15,289
The update event confirms the software
action shown here;

110
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:19,019
the next view identifies which fields are
available to inspect.

111
00:06:19,149 --> 00:06:20,999
Open Wallace Boden’s details.

112
00:06:21,489 --> 00:06:25,810
Status and Staffing shows Standing By and
Available in separate rows, with

113
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:29,189
update timestamps, and Roles lists Chief.

114
00:06:29,609 --> 00:06:33,540
This page verifies the displayed response
and staffing values; it

115
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:35,189
does not display the staffing note.

116
00:06:35,660 --> 00:06:39,640
The same careful readback helps
coordinators of community paramedicine,

117
00:06:39,849 --> 00:06:43,880
mental health response, search teams,
security shifts, wildland

118
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:47,820
crews, and industrial teams keep recorded
staffing understandable.

